  "mechanisms": [
    "The geometry of perspective: A physical surface not perpendicular to the line of sight projects a distribution of texture-elements which gets denser as the surface recedes, and whose gradient of density is proportional to the angle of slant. This retinal gradient is hypothesized to be the stimulus for perceived slant.",
    "Regularity of texture: Regular textures (repeating, adjacent, evenly-distributed elements with linear perspective) provide a more definite gradient of density, leading to clearer impressions of slant and greater differential sensitivity."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Type of texture (regular vs. irregular)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The perspective of texture, as distinguished from the perspective of rectilinear outlines, seems to be a fundamental basis for the impression of a three-dimensional environment. This suggests that manipulating texture gradients in designed environments could effectively influence the perception of surface slant and overall spatial depth."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A6_Visual_Form",
    "A3_Spatial_Config"
  ],
  "overall_theory_links": [
    "PP",
